    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1236">

                <pb facs="1217" n="931" sameAs="frus:frus1916Supp/pg931"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 867.48/312</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Chargé in Turkey</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Philip</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-06-24T17:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">June 24, 1916, 5 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2784. Your 1896, June 20.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d1236-f1"/> If Turkish Government
                    is not attempting to starve inhabitants of Lebanon, what are its objections, if
                    any, to sending relief supplies to Syria and Lebanon? Prompt reply desired.
                    Great interest in this question in United States.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
